---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { site } from '../config';

const tools = [
  {
    href: '/repair-fit-file/',
    name: 'Repair a FIT file',
    text: 'Recover a recording that was cut off, damaged in the middle, or will not upload. Rebuilds the missing summary and checksum.',
    types: '.fit',
  },
  {
    href: '/merge-fit-files/',
    name: 'Join activities',
    text: 'Turn a ride or run that was split into several recordings back into one activity.',
    types: '.fit  .gpx',
  },
  {
    href: '/fit-to-gpx/',
    name: 'FIT to GPX',
    text: 'Get a GPX track, with heart rate, cadence and power, out of a device recording.',
    types: '.fit → .gpx',
  },
  {
    href: '/gpx-to-fit/',
    name: 'GPX to FIT',
    text: 'Turn a recorded GPX track into a FIT activity file.',
    types: '.gpx → .fit',
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title={`${site.name}: repair, join and convert FIT and GPX files in your browser`}
  description="Free tools for Garmin, Wahoo and Strava activity files. Repair a corrupt FIT file, join split activities, convert between FIT and GPX. Files stay on your device."
  schema={{
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebSite',
    name: site.name,
    description: site.tagline,
  }}
>
  <h1>Fix activity files without handing them to anyone.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Repair a recording your watch did not finish saving, join a ride that got split in two, or convert between FIT and
    GPX. The work is done by your browser, on your device. The files are never uploaded.
  </p>

  <ol class="tools">
    {
      tools.map((t) => (
        <li>
          <a href={t.href}>
            <span class="t-name">{t.name}</span>
            <span class="t-text">{t.text}</span>
            <span class="t-types">{t.types}</span>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
      When you choose a file, the page reads it directly from your disk, processes it in this tab, and gives you the
      result as a download. There is no server step, so there is no upload limit, no queue and no account. You can
      load a tool page, switch off your connection, and it will still work.
    </p>
    <p>
      An activity file holds where you were, second by second, usually starting and ending at your front door. That is
      a good reason not to send it to a website you found five minutes ago, and it is the reason this one is built
      the way it is.
    </p>

    <h2>What it will not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Recover data that was never written. If the device lost power at kilometre 30, the repaired file ends at
        kilometre 30.
      </li>
      <li>
        Blend two recordings of the same activity, such as heart rate from a watch into a ride from a bike computer.
        Joining is for recordings made one after another.
      </li>
      <li>Upload anything to Garmin Connect or Strava for you. You get a file; you choose where it goes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How the output is checked</h2>
    <p>
      The code is tested against real recordings from Garmin watches and bike computers. Files the tools write are read back by a
      separate, independently written FIT decoder running in strict mode, which rejects a file with a wrong checksum
      or an incomplete record. That shows the files are structurally valid FIT. It does not guarantee how any
      particular platform will treat them, so look over the summary the tool shows you before you upload.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .tools {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .tools a {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 13rem 1fr 7.5rem;
    gap: 0.25rem 1.5rem;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding: 1.1rem 0.25rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .tools a:hover {
    color: inherit;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 7%, transparent);
  }
  .t-name {
    font: 600 1.3rem var(--serif);
  }
  .tools a:hover .t-name {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .t-text {
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .t-types {
    font: 0.82rem var(--mono);
    color: var(--muted);
    white-space: pre;
    text-align: right;
  }
  @media (max-width: 44rem) {
    .tools a {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .t-types {
      text-align: left;
    }
  }
</style>
